## P-015: Single-Artifact Shipment Closure (No Cascade Ship)

| Field      | Value                                             |
|------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| Policy ID  | P-015                                             |
| Applies To | `ship`, `shipment-reconcile` skill                |
| Gate Point | Ship Step 6 post-merge closure (shipment close)   |

**Applies when**: `{{FEATURE_SHIPMENTS}}` is true and shipments may cover a partial subset of a covering feature's tasks (partial-feature shipments).

**Statement**: Shipment closure is a flat-manifest operation. `manifest_scope(S)` is the manifest's explicit item IDs, `closure_scope(S) = items(S) ∪ {S}`, and ancestry never expands that closure scope. The ship agent MUST use the `shipment-reconcile` safe-close procedure as the default path: archive only the manifest's explicit item IDs, one artifact at a time, and then archive only the shipment record itself as its own single artifact. The cascade `backlogit_ship_shipment` path is forbidden by default because it treats the shipment as a proxy for a covering feature and can mutate out-of-manifest descendants; it is permitted only when the machine-checkable invariants below prove every reachable out-of-manifest descendant is engine-inert and the remainder of the contract still holds. After the selected close path completes, the separately sanctioned INV-12 step may archive validated linked deliberations individually; this is not closure scope.

**Precondition**: The shipment manifest (`items`) has been read; the observation set of out-of-manifest artifacts reachable from the covering feature has been computed; and a baseline-invariance snapshot has been captured for every observed artifact outside `closure_scope(S)`, recording its baseline location plus content hash alongside `git status --short -- "{{BACKLOG_DIRECTORY}}/"`; on the **CASCADE** path, a baseline fingerprint has additionally been captured for every disposition-set (`validated_linked_deliberations(S)`) record path. An artifact already archived, descoped, or missing at baseline is recorded as baseline state and is explicitly NOT a halt. Manifest ordering is not a closure precondition.

**Required Check (verify-after-each invariant)**: This invariant is **path-specific**, not one universal rule. On the **safe-close** path, after each mutation, compare the current state of every observed artifact outside `closure_scope(S)` against its baseline fingerprint. On the **CASCADE** path, after each mutation, compare the current state of every observed artifact outside `allowed_ids(S)` **and** every disposition-set (`validated_linked_deliberations(S)`) record path against its baseline fingerprint instead; a disposition-set member that changes during the cascade is engine drift, because the cascade never transitions a linked deliberation. In both cases, any change relative to baseline — location change, content-hash change, deletion, rename, new archive presence, or `parent_id` drift — is a cascade signal. The check watches for change; it does not require queue presence at baseline or after each step.

**Postcondition** (two parts): (a) **Close-path gate postcondition** (INV-10; flat sets; evaluated before INV-12 disposition): Safe-close archives only `closure_scope(S)`, preserves byte-identical baseline state for every artifact outside `allowed_ids(S)`, and leaves the shipment record archived with `archived_status: shipped`. When `CASCADE` is selected, the live postcondition gate remains `archived_ids ⊆ allowed_ids(S)` and `required_ids(S) ⊆ archived_ids`; no artifact outside `allowed_ids(S)` may be created, modified, moved, or deleted. (b) **INV-12 postcondition** (after the gate): only disposition-set deliberations whose INV-12 outcome is `archived` may change relative to the disposition baseline, each one verified immediately after its single-artifact archive; every other disposition-set member and every other artifact outside `allowed_ids(S)` stays byte-identical to its baseline.

**Violation Action (approval-gated rollback)**: If any artifact outside the Required Check's applicable scope for the active path (`closure_scope(S)` for safe-close, `allowed_ids(S)` for CASCADE) changes relative to baseline, follow this exact sequence and do nothing else: capture evidence → HALT → emit P-005 → request EXPLICIT operator approval → REVALIDATE the approved paths and current state → execute ONLY the approved rollback. Automatic, immediate, and notification-only destructive revert language is prohibited. A failed INV-12 verification follows the same sequence under `HALT — linked-deliberation disposition failed {id}`; the already-completed closure is never rolled back automatically.

**Relationship to P-007**: P-015 complements **P-007** (backlogit archive integrity after shipment). P-007 addresses archive-file deletion quirks during archival. P-015 constrains shipment closure so out-of-manifest artifacts remain outside the permitted mutation set and any baseline drift outside `allowed_ids(S)` halts the run. The post-mode deleted-file guard treats a verified INV-12 `archived` disposition move (the deliberation's queue file removed and its archive file created by the single-artifact archive) as expected, never as archive-integrity drift.

**SUPERSESSION NOTE (2026-09-15, amended 2026-09-16).** The withdrawn hierarchical-closure reading and the withdrawn `TERMINAL_CLOSE` design are retained as **SUPERSEDED PROVENANCE ONLY**. See `docs/decisions/2026-09-15-173-s-terminal-shipment-closure-deliberation.md` for that superseded provenance. The existing 155-S supersession note below remains authoritative and unchanged.

**SUPERSESSION NOTE (2026-09-29).** The 155-S linked-deliberation allowance — `allowed_ids(S)` widened by the union with `validated_linked_deliberations(S)`, and a not-yet-archived linked deliberation counted in `required_ids(S)` — was correct for backlogit 1.10.x and is superseded for 1.11.x. Backlogit commit `5a4b70dd` (v1.11.0) stopped the cascade from archiving linked deliberations that are not explicit members; its engine test `TestUArchiveCandidateFlat_UnlistedLinkedDeliberationIsUntouched` pins that behavior. The 190-S closure incident exposed the drift: the cascade left an unlisted linked deliberation unarchived, the two-set gate reported it as a missing `required_ids(S)` member, and the run halted with P-005. Linked deliberations are now outside both postcondition sets, and their disposition is INV-12.

**Evidence-class note.** CLASSIFIER LAW and ENGINE LAW are disjoint. Any invocation of this exception MUST cite the specific path-scoped comparison that verifies Proposition (1) for this workspace's own artifacts — for example, `git diff --stat <verified-commit-a> <verified-commit-b> -- <archived-artifact-path-a> <archived-artifact-path-b>` returning empty, and `git rev-parse <verified-commit-a>:<path>` / `git rev-parse <verified-commit-b>:<path>` returning identical blob OIDs for each path. `returned_ids` contents and `parent_id` clearing remain indicative and unproven rationale only; they may never be stated as measured fact and may never authorize `CASCADE`. Under the verified engine-semantics line the exception rests on two engine propositions: (1) **inert archived descendants** — an already-archived out-of-manifest `parent_id` descendant is left untouched by the cascade, proven by the path-scoped comparison above; and (2) **backlogit 1.11.x leaves linked deliberations independent** — the cascade never archives a linked deliberation that is not an explicit manifest member, proven by the engine source at `v1.11.0` (L716-751), the engine test `TestUArchiveCandidateFlat_UnlistedLinkedDeliberationIsUntouched`, and the 190-S closure observation. `archive_item` single-artifact semantics is a verified-line **assumption**, not a third proven proposition; its generalization is owned by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog (the backlogit engine-behavior compatibility registry).

#### D1a — Scope vocabulary

| Set | Definition | Role |
|---|---|---|
| **`manifest_scope(S)`** | exactly `items(S)` | what is delivered. Membership is explicit and exhaustive. |
| **`closure_scope(S)`** | `items(S) ∪ {S}` | what this closure is about and the only artifacts it may deliberately transition. |
| **`allowed_ids(S)`** | `closure_scope(S)` | the maximal permitted postcondition set. It is flat: linked deliberations never enter it; their disposition is INV-12. |
| **`required_ids(S)`** | `{ S } ∪ { qualifying feature members of S } ∪ { x ∈ items(S) : x is not already truly archived in the pre-close snapshot }`, over every manifest item regardless of its `artifact_type` — the shipment record and every qualifying feature member are unconditionally required regardless of their own pre-close declared status; every other manifest item is required only when it was not already truly archived pre-close; see item 7 below and the `shipment-reconcile` skill's Cascade Close Sub-Procedure (step 3) for the full rationale | the minimal mandatory postcondition set. |

`validated_linked_deliberations(S)` is the **disposition set**, exactly as the read-only disposition planner computes it (self-hosting implementation: `compute_linked_deliberation_disposition`): every deliberation record (`artifact_type: deliberation`) linked from any explicit member of `items(S)`, regardless of that member's `artifact_type`, through the three autoharness-defined link sources (frozen from backlogit 1.10.x `linkedDeliberationIDs`; the 1.11.x engine no longer uses them for archival): (1) the member's literal `source_deliberation_id`; (2) its Markdown description body; and (3) its references — each entry of the member's frontmatter `references` list. The description body and each `references` entry are scanned as text with the planner's deliberation-ID matcher. The disposition set excludes self-references and every ID in `closure_scope(S)` (H10): a deliberation that is itself an explicit manifest member is an ordinary `closure_scope(S)` member and never a disposition-set member. Existence is validated before location: a linked ID must first resolve to a deliberation record before any `queue/`/`archive/` location rule applies, and an ID that resolves to no such record is reported as an unresolved reference and never halts closure. Reachability never runs through `parent_id`, ancestry, or sibling walking. The disposition set enters neither `allowed_ids(S)` nor `required_ids(S)`, and never enters `manifest_scope(S)` or `closure_scope(S)`; its members are accounted for only by INV-12.

**Engine-semantics precondition (CASCADE).** `CASCADE` additionally requires verified engine semantics: the close-path selection (self-hosting implementation: `select_close_path`) must return `CASCADE` for the classifier verdict together with the engine-semantics decision. The self-hosting implementation obtains that decision from `assess_cascade_engine_semantics`; any other workspace uses an equivalent check — a fresh probe of the installed backlogit version on the same surface (MCP or CLI) that the close path will invoke, released builds only — and the `shipment-reconcile` skill records the `probe_surface`, `version`, and `commit` it observed. Verified engine-semantics lines: `1.11`. Any other probe result (an unverified engine line, an unreleased or unparseable build, a stale probe, or a probe of a different surface) selects `SAFE_CLOSE` with reason `ENGINE_SEMANTICS_UNVERIFIED`.

**SAFE_CLOSE reliance.** `SAFE_CLOSE` does not depend on cascade semantics: it fails closed and never invokes the cascade. It depends on `archive_item` semantics (the single-artifact, non-cascading archive), which is a verified-line assumption rather than a proven proposition, tracked by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog (the backlogit engine-behavior compatibility registry).

#### Invariants

* **INV-1 (Flat closure scope).** `closure_scope(S) = items(S) ∪ {S}`. Ancestry never adds a member. Linked deliberations do not extend `closure_scope(S)` or `allowed_ids(S)`; they are accounted for only by INV-12.
* **INV-2 (Membership is explicit and exhaustive).** An artifact is in `manifest_scope(S)` iff its ID appears in `items(S)`. Absence from `items(S)` is complete evidence that the artifact is not part of this delivery.
* **INV-3 (Exclusion is never a gate).** An out-of-manifest descendant may be `queued`, `active`, `blocked`, `done`, `archived`, descoped, or absent, and ancestry alone never makes it a closure blocker or a closure-scope member.
* **INV-4 (Multi-shipment feature delivery).** A feature MAY be delivered across `S1 … Sn`. `S1..Sn-1` carry no feature member; exactly one shipment, the final `Sn`, carries the feature itself as a manifest entry. This is contract-complete but operationally blocked until INV-11's external runtime prerequisite lands (tracked by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog).
* **INV-5 (Feature terminality precondition).** The final shipment may carry the feature only when every task actually delivered by `S1..Sn` is terminal. Tasks excluded from every manifest in the sequence are not part of this precondition. This is contract-complete but operationally blocked until INV-11's external runtime prerequisite lands (tracked by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog).
* **INV-6 (Engine-inertness containment gate).** `CASCADE` is permitted only when every artifact in the `parent_id` descendant set the classifier enumerates from each qualifying root feature member — i.e., every artifact transitively reachable from that feature via `parent_id`, exactly the set `classify_shipment_close_path` returns as `out_of_manifest_descendant_ids` — that lies outside `closure_scope(S)` is engine-inert. This gate is scoped precisely to that `parent_id` descendant set and never to `validated_linked_deliberations(S)`: under the verified engine line the engine leaves a linked deliberation independent — the cascade neither transitions nor archives it — so this gate never forces `SAFE_CLOSE` on that account; the linked deliberation's disposition is INV-12. Engine inertness requires the record's own parsed frontmatter value to satisfy `isinstance(status, str) and status == "archived"`. This is an exact parsed-scalar match: no .lower(), no .strip(), no casefold, no alias table, and no str() coercion may broaden it. Non-string parses such as `status: yes` or a bare `status:` fail closed and force `SAFE_CLOSE`; `Archived`, `ARCHIVED`, and the YAML-quoted literal `status: " archived "` also force `SAFE_CLOSE`. Because the comparison happens after YAML parsing, lexically distinct but YAML-equivalent values that parse to exactly `"archived"` are treated as inert; that is correct because the engine also YAML-parses the field. Any id that resolves to more than one record, whether torn across `queue/` + `archive/` or duplicated within a single root, fails closed and forces `SAFE_CLOSE`; a torn or duplicate out-of-manifest descendant can never satisfy the inertness predicate because its declared status is ambiguous.
* **INV-7 (Baseline invariance replaces baseline presence).** Safe-close records each observed artifact's baseline location plus content hash and requires that nothing outside `closure_scope(S)` changes relative to baseline. An artifact already archived, descoped, or missing at baseline is recorded as baseline state and is explicitly NOT a halt. Temporal scope: baseline invariance is evaluated at the close-path gate, before INV-12 disposition; INV-12 then applies its own invariance check against the disposition baseline.
* **INV-8 (Manifest ordering is a Stage assembly convention, NOT a closure precondition).** Stage may enforce feature-last ordering when assembling new shipments, but Ship MUST NOT treat manifest ordering as a closure precondition.
* **INV-9 (DAG orthogonality).** Shipment sequencing and pipeline topology are orthogonal to closure scope. Closure does not consult the DAG when deciding what this shipment may transition.
* **INV-10 (Close-path gate postconditions (evaluated before INV-12 disposition)).** At the close-path gate, `archived_ids ⊆ allowed_ids(S)`; `required_ids(S) ⊆ archived_ids`; every artifact outside `allowed_ids(S)` is byte-identical to baseline, including `parent_id`; and the shipment record declares `status: archived` plus `archived_status: shipped`. INV-12's `archived` deliberations are the only artifacts outside `allowed_ids(S)` that may change after the gate, and only through INV-12.
* **INV-11 (External runtime prerequisite).** Multi-shipment delivery is contract-complete but operationally blocked under backlogit 1.10.1 (the observed line; not re-verified for 1.11; re-verification is owned by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog (the backlogit engine-behavior compatibility registry)). An intermediate shipment carries no feature member, cannot qualify for `CASCADE`, and is therefore a genuine `SAFE_CLOSE` shipment. If `backlogit move <shipment_id> --status shipped` is refused (exit 9) while the manifest is not cascade-eligible, Ship MUST halt with `RECONCILE_FAIL_NO_SAFE_RECORD_TRANSITION`. The cascade path must NOT be substituted, because `returned_ids` and `parent_id` clearing are indicative and unproven only, and archiving an active shipment record stamps `archived_status: active` rather than `archived_status: shipped`. This is an unresolved upstream tooling gap tracked by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog; no silent workaround, no retry, no hand-edit.
* **INV-12 (Linked-deliberation disposition).** INV-12 is the invariant rule; `LinkedDeliberationOutcome` is the closed enum of the values it assigns. After the selected close path's gate passes, the Linked-Deliberation Disposition step assigns each disposition-set member exactly one `LinkedDeliberationOutcome`: `archived`, `already-archived`, `retained_read_error`, `retained_engine_unverified`, `retained_ambiguous`, `retained_live_status`, `retained_shared_reference`, or `retained_description_mention`. Each outcome carries a `reason_code`, which defaults to the outcome value; `retained_read_error` also carries the workspace-relative `path` and a read-error `reason_code` (`path_escape`, `symlink_or_reparse_point`, `unreadable_file`, `malformed_frontmatter`, `body_unseparable`, `malformed_stash_entry`). The enum is closed; the `reason_code` vocabulary is extensible, and consumers accept unknown reason codes. A read or containment failure yields `retained_read_error` first, before any later classification. A disposition-set member is mutated only by a single-artifact, non-cascading archive, and only when all of these hold: the engine-semantics verdict is VERIFIED; the deliberation is linked from an explicit member's `source_deliberation_id`; its own status is not live (`active`, `blocked`, `review`); it resolves to exactly one record; and it has no live referrer (a work item, the items or description of another shipment that is not truly archived, or an active stash entry). A content-hash re-check and a guard re-check run immediately before each archive, and verify-after-each runs immediately after it. The step never widens `closure_scope(S)`, is independent of the selected close path, and reports retained outcomes, which never halt; a verification failure halts with P-005 and never advances to post-mode.

7. **Pre-archived manifest members do not disqualify this exception.** A manifest member (task or feature) already present in `{{BACKLOG_DIRECTORY}}/archive/` when these preconditions are evaluated is expected and tolerated: it satisfies precondition 1's coverage/root checks the same as a `queued` member (both `queue/` and `archive/` are scanned), and its pre-archived state does not disqualify the cascade close path, does not constitute an unresolved precondition, and does not authorize falling back to the default single-artifact safe-close procedure. `archived_ids` is a **transition log**: it reports the artifacts **actually transitioned** to archived during that invocation. A non-feature manifest member (any explicit manifest member that is not a qualifying feature member) whose declared `status` is already truly `archived` before the invocation has no transition to report and is **correctly absent** from `archived_ids` — this is expected, not an anomaly, and never disqualifies the `CASCADE` verdict. **This never extends to the shipment record or to a qualifying feature member itself** (155-S, PR #407 review, thread PRRT_kwDORzpWpM6b0kjI): both remain unconditionally required regardless of their own pre-close declared status, as stated later in this item — an already-archived qualifying feature is never "correctly absent" the way a non-feature manifest member can be. The live fail-closed guard over this result is the two-set `allowed_ids` / `required_ids` gate specified in the `shipment-reconcile` skill's Cascade Close Sub-Procedure (step 3): `archived_ids` must never contain an ID outside `allowed_ids`, and every `allowed_ids` member that was **not** truly `status: archived` in the Step 0(b)/0(c) pre-close snapshot must appear in `archived_ids`. **The shipment record is a `required_ids` member unconditionally, regardless of its own pre-close declared status** — this policy summary and the skill's binding rule are the same contract and MUST NOT diverge: a pre-close shipment record that itself declared `status: archived` (an anomalous state for an artifact this same closure step is actively transitioning to `shipped`) does not satisfy this exception's checks merely by that declared status, and its absence from `archived_ids` still fails the missing-required-artifact check exactly as any other missing `required_ids` member would; no pre-close status ever exempts the shipment record from this requirement. **The same unconditional-required_ids rule applies to every qualifying feature member** (155-S, PR #407 review): Backlogit's own `ShipShipment` engine unconditionally forces every explicit qualifying feature member through `status: done` (this forcing rationale is the observed backlogit 1.10 line; not re-verified for 1.11; re-verification is owned by a durable active stash entry local to this workspace's own backlog (the backlogit engine-behavior compatibility registry)) before the cascade's archive-candidate collection (`collectArchiveCandidateIDs`, which under backlogit 1.11.x collects explicit members and their `parent_id` descendants and appends no linked deliberations) ever runs, regardless of that feature's own pre-close declared status — including an already truly `status: archived` one — so a qualifying feature member is never still `status: archived` by the time `archived_ids` is computed, unlike a non-feature manifest member, which is not forced through any such unconditional pre-collection status change. Its absence from `archived_ids` therefore always fails the missing-required-artifact check exactly as a missing shipment record would; no pre-close status ever exempts a qualifying feature member from this requirement either. Once this classification returns `CASCADE`, that close path is fixed for this shipment's closure: substituting the default single-artifact safe-close procedure between the classification and the cascade invocation is a **P-005 process deviation**, never a permitted fallback, regardless of the manifest's archival state -- this no-substitution rule applies identically whether or not any manifest member happens to be pre-archived.

   **SUPERSESSION NOTE (155-S, 2026-08-29).** This item previously asserted that the cascade operation "returns \[pre-archived members] in its `archived_ids` result exactly as it does newly-archived members", and pinned an exact-match, full-set-equality post-condition against the full manifest as one "which must never be relaxed to exclude them". **That claim was false and is WITHDRAWN.** Reading the backlogit engine source (`internal/core/shipment_lifecycle.go` `archiveItems()`) shows a truly `status: archived` item is skipped and never appended to the slice that becomes `archived_ids` — it has no transition to report. The claim was never true of the engine; the black-box spike that appeared to corroborate it (`docs/spikes/2026-08-18-cascade-close-pre-archived-member-behavior.md`, since superseded) had in fact only ever exercised inputs relocated to `status: done`, never a genuinely archived input. The two safety properties the withdrawn clause protected — nothing out-of-scope is archived, and nothing that still needed archiving is skipped — are now carried, at full strength, by the two-set `allowed_ids` / `required_ids` gate described above and specified in full in the `shipment-reconcile` skill.

This exception is defined entirely in terms of the general shape above (flat manifest scope, root-ness, engine-inertness, baseline invariance, and the postcondition sets). It MUST NOT be narrowed or special-cased to any particular feature ID, shipment ID, or manifest shape anywhere in this policy text or in any implementation of it. The machine-checkable precondition check is implemented as a pure classification function that Ship consults to select the close path — the close path is never chosen from prose judgment alone.
